\v 11 “\add Yes,\add* my¦145989 master¦145977 the¦145973 king¦145973,” Tsiva¦145970 \add ≈responded¦145969\add*. “Your servant¦145981 will¦145976 do¦145983 everything you've commanded¦145976.”
\p \add So from then on,\add* Mefiboshet always ate¦145986 at the king's table¦145989 as if he¦145983 was¦145986 one¦145990 of¦145992 the king's sons¦145991.
\v 12 Mefiboshet had a young¦145997 son¦145995 named Mika. All¦146000 \add ≈Tsiva's¦146004 household¦146002\add* became Mefiboshet's servants¦146005,
\v 13 \add ≈but¦146008\add* Mefiboshet (who was¦146009 crippled in both¦146021 legs) lived in Yerushalem¦146010 because¦146011 he \add ≈had a permanent invitation to eat¦146018 with¦146010 the¦146021 king¦146015\add*.
\c 10
