\p
\v 1 \add ≈Some time later,\add* the¦146030 \add ≈Ammonite\add* king died¦146029, and¦146033 his¦146036 son¦146035 Hanun¦146034 replace him¦146036 as king.
\v 2 “I'll¦146040 be kind to Nahash's¦146049 son¦146047 Hanun,” David¦146039 said¦146038, “just¦146050 like his father¦146052 was kind to me¦146053.” So¦146038 \add *he¦146055\add* sent¦146055 servants¦146060 \add to Hanun\add* \add ≈to convey his condolences\add*.
\p \add ≈When¦146038\add* \add *they¦146064\add* \add ≈arrived in¦146058 the Ammon¦146069 region¦146067\add*,
\v 3 the \add ≈Ammonite commanders\add* said¦146071 to their¦146079 master¦146079 Hanun¦146078, “\add ≈Do you¦146104 think that David¦146081 really sent¦146088 condolences to honour¦146080 your¦146085 late father¦146084\add*? Isn't it \add more likely\add* \add ≈because he wanted them to spy¦146097 on¦146085 us, and¦146071 to explore¦146093 the city¦146096 so he could more easily conquer it\add*?”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So¦146106\add* Hanun¦146107 \add ≈seized\add* David's¦146111 servants¦146110 and had half of their beards¦146116 shaved¦146112 off and their robes¦146120 cut¦146117 in¦146121 half \add all the way\add* up to¦146122 their buttocks¦146123, then¦146106 he¦146124 sent¦146124 them¦146124 off.
