\v 5 “\add ≈Go to bed¦147676\add* and pretend¦147677 to be sick¦147677,” Yonadab¦147672 \add ≈suggested\add*. “Then¦147670 \add when\add* your¦147679 father¦147679 comes¦147678 to see¦147680 you, \add ≈ask\add* him¦147682, ‘Please¦147684 let¦147683 my sister¦147686 Tamar¦147685 come¦147683 \add ≈to look after my food. She can cook it here and serve it to me\add*.’ ”
\v 6 \add ≈So¦147700\add* Amnon lay¦147700 down¦147700 and pretended¦147702 to be sick¦147702, and \add when\add* the¦147704 king¦147704 came¦147703 to see¦147705 him, Amnon \add ≈asked\add* \add *him\add*, “Please¦147713, let my sister¦147715 Tamar¦147714 come¦147711 and make¦147716 a couple of bread¦147716 rolls \add ≈while I'm watching, and then¦147700 she can serve them to me\add*.”
\p
\v 7 \add ≈So¦147723\add* David¦147724 sent¦147723 \add a message\add* to Tamar¦147727 \add ≈to ask her to\add* go¦147730 to her brother¦147734 Amnon's¦147733 house¦147732 and¦147723 prepare¦147735 food¦147738 for him¦147737.
\v 8 \add ≈So¦147740\add* Tamar¦147741 went¦147740 to her¦147744 half-brother Amnon's¦147743 house¦147742 where he¦147745 was¦147746 lying¦147746 down¦147746. She¦147740 took¦147747 \add ≈some\add* dough¦147750 and kneaded it¦147747 into breadrolls and baked¦147755 them.
\v 9 Then¦147760 she¦147764 took¦147760 the¦147763 tray \add ≈over to him¦147765\add*, but¦147760 he¦147766 refused¦147766 to eat¦147767, saying¦147768, “\add ≈Send¦147761 everyone else out¦147770 of here\add*,” \add ≈so they¦147775 all left¦147764\add*.
\v 10 Then¦147781 Amnon told Tamar, “Bring¦147786 the food¦147787 into the bedroom so¦147789 that \add ≈you can serve it¦147791 to me\add*.” \add ≈So¦147781\add* Tamar took the breadrolls that she'd made¦147797, and¦147781 took them¦147798 to Amnon in the bedroom.
\v 11 \add ≈But¦147803 when she¦147803 got close enough to¦147804 him¦147804,\add* he grabbed her¦147808 and insisted, “Come¦147814 \add and\add* lie¦147812 down with¦147808 me¦147813, my sister¦147814.”
\p
\v 12 “No, my brother¦147820,” she told him, “don't humiliate me¦147823 because¦147824 \add ≈we Israelis¦147829 don't do that¦147836\add*. Don't¦147821 do this¦147836 disgraceful¦147835 thing¦147835.
\v 13 Where¦147839 \add ≈could I hide my¦147843 shame\add*? And¦147844 you—you'd be¦147845 \add ≈considered\add* a godless fool in¦147848 Yisrael¦147848. \add ≈Just speak¦147850 to the¦147855 king¦147855 because¦147856 he'd give me¦147858 to you to marry\add*.”
\v 14 \add ≈But¦147869 he didn't¦147861 want her advice, and being\add* stronger¦147865 than her, he humiliated her \add ≈by forcing her down and raping\add* her.
\p
\v 15 \add ≈Instantly¦147887,\add* Amnon¦147872 hated¦147871 her with terrible hatred—a much greater hatred than¦147881 the¦147881 love¦147881 with which¦147882 he'd loved¦147883 her, and \add *he\add* told her to get up¦147888 and get out.
\p
\v 16 “No, \add no\add*.” she responded¦147891. “\add ≈Sending me away would be even worse than what you¦147902 just\add* did¦147902 to¦147904 me.”
\p \add ≈But¦147891\add* he¦147906 refused to listen¦147907 to her¦147908
\v 17 and called¦147910 his¦147914 \add ≈personal servant\add*. He¦147915 told him¦147914 to¦147923 \add ≈take\add* the woman away and send¦147916 her outside¦147923 and then¦147910 lock¦147924 the door¦147925 behind¦147926 her.
\v 18 (She¦147940 was¦147928 wearing the kind of long¦147929 robe that the virgin¦147937 daughters¦147934 of the king¦147936 wore¦147933 as an outer garment¦147929.)
\p \add ≈So¦147928 the servant¦147941 pushed\add* her outside¦147942 and¦147928 locked¦147943 the door¦147944 behind¦147945 her.
\v 19 Then¦147947 Tamar¦147948 put ashes on her head and tore¦147957 the long¦147953 robe¦147953 that¦147955 she was wearing. She¦147958 put her hand on her head and wailed as she walked \add home\add*.\f + \fr 13:19 \ft The ashes, tearing of clothes, putting hand on head, and wailing, were all cultural signs of mourning and distress.\f*
\v 20 Her¦147970 brother Abshalom \add ≈asked\add* her, “\add ≈Did your half-brother Amnon¦147971 mess\add* with¦147974 you? But¦147967 now¦147975, my sister¦147976, \add ≈don't tell anyone since he's¦147979 your brother and¦147967 don't let it upset you\add*.” \add ≈So¦147967\add* Tamar¦147989 lived \add ≈in a state of¦147993 desolation¦147990\add* in her brother Abshalom's¦147969 house¦147991.
\p
\v 21 \add ≈When¦147995\add* King¦147995 David¦147996 heard¦147997 about¦147999 all that, \add ≈he got very angry\add*.
\v 22 \add Also,\add* Abshalom¦148010 said nothing to¦148014 Amnon¦148013 (either¦148014 good¦148017 or bad¦148014) because¦148018 he hated¦148020 \add *him\add* for humiliating his¦148032 sister¦148032 Tamar¦148031.
\s1 Abshalom kills Amnon
