\c 14
\s1 Abshalom returns to Yerushalem
\rem /s1 Joab Arranges for Absalom's Return; Absalom Returns to Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add ≈Now\add* Yoav¦148399 (Tseruyah's¦148402 son¦148400) \add ≈realised\add* that¦148403 the¦148405 king¦148406 \add ≈missed\add* Abshalom¦148409,
\v 2 \add ≈so\add* he sent¦148411 for a wise woman from Tekoa¦148413 and told her¦148419, “Now, please¦148422 put on mourning clothes¦148426 and don't¦148429 \add ≈put on any make-up\add*. \add ≈Act\add* like¦148434 a woman who¦148438 has¦148438 been¦148438 mourning for the dead¦148441 for \add ≈several\add* days¦148436,
\v 3 and go¦148443 to the king and \add ≈give him¦148448 this¦148450 message¦148449\add*.” Then¦148443 Yoav¦148452 told her¦148456 what to say to the king.
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So¦148458\add* the Tekoan woman¦148459 went to the king¦148463 and¦148458 \add ≈fell¦148464 onto her¦148467 knees with his face¦148467 down¦148469 to the ground\add*, saying¦148458, “\add ≈Your majesty, help\add* \add me\add*.”
\p
\v 5 “What's \add ≈the matter\add*?” the king¦148478 \add ≈asked\add*.
\p “Alas, I'm a widow¦148486,” she¦148482 \add ≈replied\add*. “My¦148489 husband¦148489 has died¦148488.
\v 6 Your¦148491 female servant¦148491 had two sons¦148493, and the two of¦148495 them fought¦148494 \add each other out\add* in¦148496 the countryside¦148496. There¦148497 wasn't¦148497 anyone¦148501 there¦148497 who could \add ≈stop\add* them, and then¦148491 one¦148501 \add son\add* struck¦148500 the other and killed¦148505 him¦148500.
\v 7 \add ≈Now\add* listen, the entire¦148510 clan¦148512 has¦148509 \add ≈turned against\add* your¦148515 female servant¦148515 and \add ≈demanded\add*, ‘Give \add <us\add* the one¦148521 who¦148521 \add ≈killed\add* his¦148522 brother, and we'll kill¦148528 him¦148523 in¦148524 exchange for¦148524 the life¦148524 of¦148524 the brother that he¦148527 killed. Also¦148508, \add ≈it would mean that this evil thing couldn't be passed on¦148524\add*.’ \add ≈But¦148508 that would leave¦148538 me with¦148524 no descendants to continue\add* my¦148543 husband's¦148543 name¦148544.”
\p
\v 8 “Go¦148559 \add ≈home\add*,” the king¦148553 told the woman¦148556, “and I'll¦148560 \add ≈make a decision about¦148558 what to do\add*.”
\p
\v 9 \add ≈But¦148563\add* the Tekoan woman¦148564 \add ≈continued¦148563\add*, “The¦148565 \add ≈blame\add*,\f + \fr 14:9 \ft Presumably for not putting the murderer to death.\f* my master¦148570 the king, \add ≈will be on¦148569 me¦148569 and my extended family, but¦148563\add* the king and his¦148578 throne¦148578 will be innocent¦148579.”
\p
\v 10 “Anyone¦148584 who¦148584 speaks¦148584 against¦148591 you,” said¦148582 the king¦148583, “bring¦148586 them to¦148587 me¦148587, and¦148588 then they won't¦148588 \add ≈trouble\add* you any more.”
\p
\v 11 “Please¦148598,” she¦148595 \add ≈insisted\add*, “may the king¦148599 \add ≈ask\add* your¦148603 god¦148603 Yahweh¦148602 to¦148623 prevent the avenger¦148606 of¦148606 blood¦148607 from increasing¦148604 the \add ≈tragedy\add*, so my¦148613 son¦148613 won't¦148609 be destroyed¦148610.”
\p “As surely as Yahweh lives,” he¦148614 \add ≈vowed\add*, “not¦148609 a hair from your son's¦148622 head will¦148620 fall¦148620 to the¦148599 ground¦148623.”
\p
\v 12 Then¦148625 the woman¦148626 said¦148625, “Please¦148629, let¦148627 your¦148630 female servant¦148630 \add ≈ask\add* my¦148633 master¦148633 the king¦148634 \add ≈a question\add*.”
\p “Speak¦148637,” he¦148636 \add ≈replied\add*.
\p
\v 13 \add ≈So\add* the woman¦148641 \add ≈asked\add*, “Why have¦148643 you¦148643 \add ≈acted\add* like¦148644 this¦148644 against God's¦148648 people¦148647? When the king¦148650 says¦148640 that¦148644 \add to me\add*, isn't¦148654 he convicting himself since he hasn't \add ≈brought¦148655 back¦148655\add* \em his¦148659 own\em* \add ≈banished¦148659 son¦148659\add*?
\v 14 \add ≈It's certain¦148663 that we'll¦148664\add* \add all\add* die. Water that's spilt¦148666 on the ground can't¦148669 be gathered together \add again\add* \add ≈but¦148665\add* God's¦148674 \add not like that\add*. Rather than taking¦148673 away¦148673 life¦148675, he devises¦148676 plans¦148677 so that the one who was banished¦148681 can be \add ≈gathered back in\add*.
\v 15 \add The reason\add* that¦148695 I've¦148686 come¦148686 now¦148683 to tell this to the king my¦148691 master¦148691, is because¦148696 the people¦148698 have¦148697 frightened me. \add ≈So¦148683\add* your¦148700 servant said¦148699 \add to herself\add*, ‘I will speak¦148701, please¦148703, to the king. Perhaps¦148707 the king will \add ≈honour the request\add* of his female servant.
\v 16 \add ≈Maybe\add* the king¦148717 will listen¦148716 in order to¦148718 save his¦148721 female servant¦148721 from the man¦148723 who's \add ≈cutting\add* me¦148725 and¦148726 my¦148728 son¦148728 together¦148729 \add off\add* from the inheritance¦148730 given by God¦148731.’
\v 17 And your female servant¦148734 \add ≈thought\add*, ‘Please¦148737, let the word of¦148738 my master the king become¦148735 \add ≈my assurance\add*, because¦148743 my master the king is like God's¦148754 messenger¦148738 \add when it comes to\add* understanding good¦148751 and¦148733 evil. And may Yahweh¦148740 your god¦148754 be¦148755 with¦148756 you¦148756.’ ”\x + \xo 14:17: \xt 2Sam 19:27.\x*
\p
\v 18 “\add Hang on,\add*” the king¦148760 told the woman¦148764. “\add ≈I¦148772 want you¦148774 to answer¦148759 this question honestly\add*.”
\p “Please¦148767, let¦148777 my¦148780 master¦148780 the¦148760 king speak¦148777.” \add *she\add* \add ≈responded¦148761\add*.
\p
\v 19 “\add ≈Did Yoav¦148786 perhaps have anything to do\add* with¦148819 all¦148788 this¦148790?” he¦148783 \add ≈asked\add*.
\p The¦148792 woman¦148792 answered¦148791, “By¦148794 the life¦148794 of¦148796 your spirit, my master the king, there's¦148801 no \add ≈avoiding anything\add* that¦148790 my master the king has¦148808 said. \add Yes,\add* your servant Yoav—he himself commanded¦148816 me¦148816, and¦148783 he himself \add ≈told¦148816\add* your female servant \add ≈what I should say\add*.
\v 20 Your servant¦148834 Yoav¦148835 \add ≈set this up to try to remedy the situation¦148832\add*. But¦148840 my¦148840 master¦148840 is¦148841 wise¦148841 like¦148842 the wisdom¦148842 of one of God's messengers¦148843 to know¦148845 everything that's¦148850 \add going on here\add* on earth¦148851.”
\p
\v 21 Then the king¦148855 \add summoned\add* Yoav¦148858 and told him, “\add ≈All right then,\add* I'll \add ≈sort this¦148866 out. Now\add* go¦148867 \add and\add* bring¦148868 back¦148868 the young¦148871 man¦148871 Abshalom¦148874.”
\p
\v 22 Yoav¦148877 fell¦148876 \add ≈onto his knees and bowed¦148882\add* his face¦148880 to the ground, and blessed¦148883 the king, then¦148876 he said¦148887, “Today¦148888, your servant knows¦148890 that¦148892 I¦148894 have¦148894 found¦148894 favour¦148895 in¦148896 your eyes¦148896, my¦148897 master¦148897 the king, since the king \add ≈is going to do¦148901 what\add* his servant \add ≈asked\add*.”
\v 23 Then¦148909 Yoav¦148910 got up¦148909 and went¦148911 to Geshur¦148912 to get Abshalom¦148916 and bring¦148913 him \add back\add* to Yerushalem¦148917,
\v 24 \add ≈but\add* the king¦148921 said¦148920, “He¦148922 can go around to his house, but he's \add ≈to stay right away from me\add*.” \add ≈So\add* Abshalom¦148930 \add ≈lived in his own\add* house, \add ≈but wasn't allowed into the palace\add*.
