\p
\v 25 \add ≈Now\add* Abshalom¦148940 \add ≈was¦148943 admired as the most handsome¦148946 man¦148944\add* in¦148947 all¦148947 Yisrael¦148949—from the \add ≈top\add* of his head¦148955 to the \add ≈bottom\add* of his feet, \add ≈you wouldn't be able to find a blemish¦148960 on his skin\add*.
\v 26 He¦148962 \add ≈had his¦148965 hair cut once a year when it got too heavy¦148976 on¦148962 him¦148977, and¦148962 when the¦148982 hair was¦148976 weighed using standard weights¦148986, it would be over two¦148984 kilograms\add*.
\v 27 Abshalom¦148990 had three¦148991 sons¦148992 and¦148989 a daughter¦148993 named Tamar¦148996—a very attractive woman¦148999.
\p
\v 28 Abshalom¦149005 lived¦149004 in¦149006 Yerushalem¦149006 for two¦149007 years¦149007 \add ≈without ever getting to\add* see¦149012 the king¦149010,
\v 29 \add ≈so\add* \add *he¦149014\add* \add ≈sent a message\add* to Yoav \add ≈about getting an invitation to see\add* the¦149023 king¦149023, but \add @Yoav\add* wouldn't come to him¦149027. He¦149028 sent \add ≈another message\add*, but \add @Yoav\add* \add still\add* wouldn't come,
\v 30 \add ≈so\add* he¦149035 \add ≈instructed\add* his servants, “Listen, Yoav's \add ≈piece of¦149038 land¦149040 with¦149052 a barley crop is¦149042 nearby\add*. Go¦149049 and¦149035 set¦149050 fire to it.” \add ≈So¦149035\add* Abshalom's servants \add ≈burnt\add* \add @Yoav's barley\add*.\f + \fr 14:30 \ft TC: The Septuagint translation adds the following words here: “And Abshalom's servants burnt them up. Then Yoav's servants came to him, tearing their garments. They said…”\f*
\p
\v 31 Then Yoav¦149063 went¦149064 to¦149065 Abshalom's¦149067 house¦149068 and¦149069 \add ≈demanded\add*, “Why did your¦149073 servants¦149073 \add ≈destroy my barley crop\add*?”
