\p
\v 1 \add ≈Sometime after¦149152 all that\add*, Abshalom¦149156 \add ≈acquired\add* a chariot¦149157 and horses¦149158 and \add hired\add* fifty¦149159 men to¦149155 run¦149161 ahead¦149162 of him¦149162.
\v 2 \add *He¦149189\add* would get up early¦149164 and stand¦149166 \add ≈on the side¦149169 of the road¦149170 going in and out of the city\add*. \add ≈Every¦149201 time that¦149194 someone came¦149183 into the city to take a dispute to be settled by the king¦149186, Abshalom¦149165 would ask them where¦149192 they were from, and they'd reply with their city and tribe¦149199\add*,
\v 3 then¦149204 \add *he'd\add* say, “Listen, \add No doubt\add* what you're saying¦149204 is sensible and¦149204 right¦149210, but¦149204 the king¦149216 hasn't \add ≈appointed anyone¦149211 to listen¦149211 to people from¦149215 your¦149208 area\add*.”
\v 4 Then¦149218 Abshalom¦149219 would \add ≈add\add*, “\add ≈If I¦149238 was\add* appointed¦149222 as judge¦149223, then¦149218 every¦149227 person¦149229 with a dispute or case¦149236 could come¦149226 to¦149225 me¦149225 and¦149218 \add ≈obtain\add* justice¦149238.”
\v 5 Whenever¦149247 anyone¦149243 approached him to \add ≈bow¦149244 respectfully\add* to him, he¦149246 would put out¦149246 his¦149249 hand¦149249 and \add ≈embrace\add* them and kiss¦149252 them.
