\p
\v 7 Four¦149295\f + \fr 15:7 \ft The Hebrew says forty years, but this is interpreted here as a scribal error. (David's entire reign was only forty years, see 1 Kings 2:11.)\f* years \add ≈later,\add* Abshalom¦149283 \add ≈begged\add* the king¦149286, “Please¦149288, \add ≈release me to\add* go¦149287 and fulfill the vow¦149292 that¦149293 I¦149289 made to Yahweh¦149296 in¦149297 Hebron¦149297,
\v 8 because¦149299 when¦149304 I was living in Geshur¦149305 (in Aram¦149306) \add ≈I promised Yahweh¦149313 that\add* if¦149308 he¦149302 brought me¦149312 \add back\add* to¦149307 Yerushalem¦149314, then I'd worship¦149315 him \add in Hebron\add*.”
\p
\v 9 “Go in¦149325 peace¦149325,” the king¦149323 \add ≈responded¦149320, so\add* Abshalom \add ≈left for\add* Hebron¦149328.
\v 10 \add From there,\add* \add *he¦149331\add* sent¦149331 \add ≈sleeper groups\add* into all \add areas\add* of Yisrael¦149337, telling \add <them\add*, “When you¦149339 all hear¦149339 the¦149342 trumpet¦149343 blasts, then \add ≈shout\add*, ‘Abshalom¦149332 has¦149345 become¦149345 king¦149345 from Hebron¦149347!’ ”
\v 11 Abshalom¦149351 \add ≈had¦149356 invited¦149356\add* two¦149353 hundred¦149353 men from Yerushalem¦149355 to go with him \add to Hebron\add*, but¦149349 they were innocent¦149358 and¦149349 didn't¦149359 know¦149360 what was \add ≈about¦149358 to happen\add*.
\v 12 Then¦149365 \add while\add* he¦149365 was offering¦149375 sacrifices¦149378, Abshalom \add also\add* sent¦149365 for David's¦149372 adviser Ahitofel¦149369 from Giloh¦149374. \add ≈By now\add* the¦149378 conspiracy¦149380 was strengthening, and Abshalom \add ≈was becoming popular with¦149375 more and more\add* people¦149382.
\s1 The fleeing of David
