\p
\v 5 \add ≈When¦150001\add* King¦150002 David got to¦150004 Bahurim¦150006, wow, a man¦150009 from¦150008 Sha'ul's¦150014 \add ≈extended family¦150011\add* came out. He was Gera's¦150019 son¦150017 Shimei¦150016, and he came out and cursed¦150022 \add <David\add*.
\v 6 Then¦150024 he¦150024 threw stones¦150025 at¦150025 David¦150028 and all \add *his\add* servants¦150033, and all the people¦150038 and \add ≈warriors\add* on¦150025 \add ≈both sides\add* of \add @the king¦150034\add*.
\v 7 “Go¦150052 away, go away, you worthless¦150055 man of \add ≈much bloodshed¦150053\add*!” Shimei¦150048 \add ≈yelled\add*.
\v 8 “\add Now\add* Yahweh's returning to you all¦150060 the blood¦150062 of Sha'ul's¦150065 household¦150063. You¦150067 \add ≈took over as\add* king in¦150075 his¦150068 place¦150068, \add ≈but¦150070\add* Yahweh's giving¦150070 the kingdom into the hand¦150075 of your¦150079 son¦150077 Abshalom¦150076. Look at¦150075 you, you're evil because¦150080 you're a man¦150081 of \add ≈much bloodshed¦150082\add*.”
\p
\v 9 The¦150087 Tseruyah's¦150089 son¦150087 Abishai¦150086 \add ≈asked\add* the king, “Why should¦150102 this¦150097 dead¦150096 dog¦150095 curse¦150094 my¦150100 master¦150100 the king? Please¦150104, let¦150102 me¦150102 cross¦150102 over¦150102 and¦150085 \add ≈lop off\add* his¦150108 head¦150108.”
\p
\v 10 “What \add ≈does¦150134 it matter\add* to me, or to you¦150134 \add two\add* sons¦150117 of¦150117 Tseruyah¦150118?” the king¦150112 \add ≈replied\add*. “He's cursing¦150121 because Yahweh¦150124 told him to curse¦150127 \add *me\add*, \add ≈so¦150119 who has¦150125 any right to ask him why he's¦150121 saying¦150111 it\add*?”
\v 11 Then David¦150139 told Abishai¦150142 and all¦150145 his¦150147 servants¦150147, “Look, my \add ≈own biological son¦150149 is¦150154 trying to kill me\add*. \add ≈Now¦150161 on top of¦150162\add* that, this Benjaminite¦150164. Leave¦150165 him and let¦150167 him curse¦150167, because¦150159 Yahweh¦150172 \add ≈told him to\add*.
\v 12 Perhaps¦150174 Yahweh¦150176 will¦150175 see my¦150177 suffering and¦150179 return good¦150182 on¦150177 me¦150181, instead of this¦150186 cursing¦150184 today.”
\v 13 \add ≈As\add* David¦150189 and his¦150197 men¦150190 \add ≈continued along\add* the road¦150191, Shimei¦150193 walked along the hillside beside him—walking and cursing¦150199, and throwing stones¦150201 and dirt at¦150191 him.
\v 14 \add Later\add*, the king¦150208 and all¦150209 the people¦150211 with¦150214 him¦150214 arrived¦150207,\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Probably at the fords mentioned in 15:28.\f* and feeling very tired, they rested.
\s1 The conflicting advice of Hushay and Ahitofel
\rem /s1 The Advice of Hushai and Ahithophel; The Counsel of Ahithophel; Absalom in Jerusalem; Ahithophel Advises Absalom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 15 \add Meanwhile,\add* Abshalom¦150219 and \add ≈all¦150220 his followers\add* arrived in Yerushalem¦150226, and Ahitofel¦150227 was among \add #them\add*.
\v 16 \add ≈When¦150230\add* David's¦150237 friend¦150236 Hushay¦150234 (the Arkite¦150235) came¦150233 to¦150238 Abshalom¦150240, he¦150241 \add ≈greeted\add* \add *him\add*, “May the king live. May the king live.”
\p
\v 17 “Is¦150256 \add ≈that¦150256 how you show\add* kindness to¦150261 your¦150257 friend¦150260 \add David\add*?” Abshalom¦150252 \add ≈responded¦150251\add*. “Why didn't¦150262 you go¦150264 with¦150265 \add *him\add*?”
\p
\v 18 “No¦150274,” Hushay¦150270 \add ≈replied\add*. “I'll \add ≈serve whoever\add* Yahweh¦150278 and these people¦150279 and all¦150281 the¦150283 men of¦150283 Yisrael¦150284 have¦150277 chosen¦150277. \add So\add* I'll stay with¦150288 \add ≈you\add*.
\v 19 \add ≈Besides that\add*, who should I serve? \add ≈Why shouldn't¦150295 I serve my master's\add* son¦150297? Just¦150298 as¦150298 I've served¦150299 your¦150304 father¦150301, \add ≈similarly, I'll¦150293 serve you¦150304\add*.”
\p
\v 20 Then Abshalom¦150308 \add turned to\add* Ahitofel¦150311, “Give¦150312 us advice. What¦150315 should we¦150317 do¦150317 \add next\add*?”
\p
\v 21 “Go to your father's¦150328 slave-wives that he¦150330 left¦150330 to guard the¦150332 house¦150332,” Ahitofel¦150320 \add ≈replied\add*. “\add Lie with them\add* then all Yisrael¦150336 will hear¦150333 that you've¦150339 made¦150339 your father stink, and¦150319 then \add ≈everyone\add* with you will be \add ≈encouraged\add*.”
\v 22 \add ≈So¦150350\add* they \add ≈set up\add* a tent¦150352 for¦150351 Abshalom¦150351 on the¦150355 \add palace\add* roof¦150355 and¦150356 \add *he¦150356\add* \add ≈had sex\add* with his¦150361 father's¦150361 slave-wives \add ≈where everyone¦150350 would know what was going¦150356 on\add*.\x + \xo 16:22: \xt 2Sam 12:11-12.\x*
\p
\v 23 In¦150371 those¦150372 days¦150371, Ahitofel's¦150368 advice¦150367 was just¦150373 \add ≈as good as inquiring directly from\add* God¦150378, \add ≈so¦150379 David¦150386 in¦150377 the¦150382 past, and¦150367 now Abshalom¦150388, accepted what he¦150374 said\add*.
\c 17
\s1 Hushay argues against Ahitofel's advice
\rem /s1 Hushai Misleads Absalom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Ahitofel¦150392 \add ≈asked\add* Abshalom¦150395, “Please¦150397, let me choose¦150396 twelve thousand¦150401 men, then let me \add ≈go\add* and pursue¦150404 after¦150405 David¦150407 tonight.
\v 2 I'll \add ≈attack\add* him while \add #they're\add* tired and \add ≈lacking energy\add*. \add ≈Once I panic¦150416\add* \add #them\add*, all¦150419 the people¦150421 with¦150424 him will flee¦150418 and I'll be able to strike¦150425 the king¦150428 by himself.
\v 3 Then¦150431 I'll¦150431 \add ≈bring¦150431\add* all the people \add ≈back\add* to¦150435 you, like \add ≈a reunion\add*. You'll \add have\add* the man¦150438 you're¦150440 \add ≈wanting\add*, and¦150431 all the people will¦150445 be¦150445 in peace¦150446.”
\v 4 \add ≈That¦150456 seemed very sensible to\add* Abshalom¦150451 and¦150452 all¦150453 the¦150455 Israeli¦150455 elders¦150455.
\rem /s1 Hushai Counters Ahithophel's Advice; The Counsel of Hushai
\p
\v 5 \add ≈But¦150459\add* Abshalom¦150460 \add ≈insisted\add*, “Now, call Hushay¦150464 the¦150465 Arkite¦150465 \add ≈as well\add*, and¦150459 let's also¦150463 \add ≈listen¦150466 to¦150464 his¦150469 suggestions\add*.”
\v 6 \add ≈So¦150474 when\add* Hushay¦150475 \add ≈arrived\add*, Abshalom¦150478 \add ≈asked\add* him¦150481, “Ahitofel¦150486 has¦150485 \add ≈suggested so and¦150474 so\add*. Should¦150480 we¦150487 \add ≈do¦150487 what he¦150485 said¦150479\add*? If¦150491 not¦150493, \add ≈what would you¦150494 recommend?\add*”
\p
\v 7 “This¦150512 time¦150511, Ahitofel's¦150510 suggestion isn't¦150503 \add such\add* good¦150505 advice¦150506,” Hushay¦150499 \add ≈replied\add*.
\v 8 You yourself know¦150517 your father and his¦150523 men¦150523—\add and do remember\add* that they're \add ≈powerful warriors¦150525\add*—they're \add ≈still furious\add* like¦150530 a bear¦150530 in¦150532 the countryside¦150532 \add ≈that's been robbed¦150531 of its cubs¦150531\add*. And your father is¦150534 an \add experienced\add* man¦150534 of war¦150535, and he¦150537 won't¦150536 spend the night¦150537 \add there\add* with¦150538 the people¦150540.
\v 9 Listen, he's¦150544 \add probably\add* \add ≈already hiding¦150546 in a cavern or some other¦150550 place¦150551\add*. \add Also,\add* if \add ≈some of your men\add* were killed early \add in the fighting\add*, \add ≈then the rumour would go around that¦150562 Abshalom's¦150564 already lost many men\add*.
\v 10 Then¦150566 \add ≈even your bravest warriors might lose heart\add*, because¦150578 all¦150581 Yisrael¦150583 knows¦150580 that your father¦150587 is a \add ≈powerful warrior¦150586\add*, and¦150566 \add also\add* that the¦150588 men with¦150592 him¦150592 are \add ≈very experienced\add*.
\v 11 \add ≈So this is what I¦150595 suggest\add*: \add ≈Call warriors from\add* all¦150599 Yisrael¦150601—from Dan¦150602 \add in the far north\add* to Beer¦150605-Sheva¦150606 \add in the far south\add*. They'll be as numerous as sand¦150607 \add grains\add* on¦150616 the beach and¦150614 you¦150598 \add ≈personally¦150614 will¦150615 be able to lead them into\add* battle¦150616.
\v 12 Then¦150618 we'll¦150618 \add ≈be able to¦150619 find¦150624 him wherever¦150623 he¦150624 is¦150624\add*, and \add ≈attack him like dew¦150630 blankets the entire¦150638 area\add*, and neither him nor any of the men¦150640 with¦150620 him \add ≈will survive\add*, not even one¦150646.
\v 13 If¦150657 he \add ≈escaped into some\add* city, \add ≈we'd all¦150655\add* bring¦150654 ropes¦150662 to¦150658 that city and¦150648 drag¦150663 \add @the stones\add* into the valley¦150667 until¦150668 even a pebble¦150677 couldn't be found¦150673 there¦150674.”
\p
\v 14 Abshalom and all¦150682 the \add ≈Israeli¦150684 elders agreed that Hushay's¦150688 advice was better than¦150690 Ahitofel's\add*. (Yahweh¦150702 had¦150693 \add ≈influenced\add* them to¦150694 reject Ahitofel's good¦150699 advice so \add *he¦150693\add* could bring¦150701 disaster onto Abshalom.)
\s1 The warning to David to flee
\rem /s1 David Is Warned and Escapes; Hushai Warns David to Escape
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 15 Then Hushay¦150712 told the \add two\add* priests¦150719 Tsadok¦150715 and Evyatar what Ahitofel¦150723 had suggested to Abshalom¦150726 and the Israeli¦150728 elders¦150728 and \add ≈what he'd countered it with\add*.
\v 16 Then¦150735 \add ≈he told¦150738 them\add*, “\add ≈Get that¦150756 message to\add* David¦150739 quickly¦150737. \add Tell him\add* not to \add ≈overnight\add* at¦150745 the fords¦150745 in¦150745 the wilderness¦150746, but¦150735 to cross¦150748 over otherwise he'll be¦150751 killed \add ≈along with everyone¦150755 else\add* with him.”
\p
\v 17 \add Their sons\add* Yonatan and Ahimaats¦150760 were¦150761 waiting at¦150762 Eyn-Rogel \add ≈where\add* a female servant¦150766 would come and \add ≈pass the message onto\add* them, because¦150774 it \add ≈wouldn't be safe for them\add* to be seen¦150777 entering¦150778 the city¦150779. Then¦150759 they themselves would go¦150765 and inform King¦150772 David¦150773.
