\v 34 “How \add ≈much longer am I¦152556 likely to live\add*?” Barzillai¦152552 \add ≈pondered\add*. “\add ≈Would it really be worth me moving\add* to Yerushalem¦152559 with¦152559 the king¦152549?
\v 35 \add ≈Your servant is eighty years¦152569 old\add*. \add ≈My mind isn't the same as it was, and I can't really taste what I\add* eat or drink. \add ≈I can barely hear the sounds\add* of¦152568 the men and women singing. \add ≈So\add* why would your servant become an extra burden to my master the king¦152576?”
\v 36 \add ≈As a token privilege\add*, your servant will cross the Yordan with the king, \add ≈but¦152600\add* why should the king reward me with that¦152598 \add generous\add* reward?
\v 37 Please, let your servant return, and let me¦152632 die in my city \add ≈near\add* the graves of¦152624 my \add ≈parents\add*. \add ≈But¦152631\add* look, \add here's\add* your servant Kimham—let him cross¦152623 over¦152623 with¦152628 my master the king¦152630, and do \add ≈whatever you consider to be appropriate\add* for¦152631 him.”
\p
\v 38 “Kimham¦152651 can cross¦152652 over¦152652 with¦152643 me,” the¦152656 king¦152656 said, “and¦152657 I myself will do for him \add ≈whatever\add* \em you\em* \add ≈consider to be appropriate\add*—everything that¦152661 you choose concerning me, I'll do for you.”
\v 39 \add ≈So\add* all the people crossed the Yordan, and then the king crossed \add over\add*. The king kissed Barzillai and blessed him¦152676, and then \add @Barzillai\add* returned \add ≈home\add*.
\s1 Yehudah and Yisrael disagree
